mediacodec: a buffer let go of after its decoder closed logs with no context

A picture of a MediaCodec decoder that draws into a surface is a buffer the
decoder still owns, and whoever shows it may hold it past the decoder's own
end: the decoder keeps its MediaCodec until the last such buffer is back
(MediaCodecDecContext's reference count), and that is what the count is for.
But giving such a buffer back, with a time, without one or by freeing the
frame, writes a line to the log first, and writes it with the codec context
the decoder was opened with, which avcodec_free_context has freed by then.
av_log reads the AVClass at the head of what it is given before it looks at
the level, so the line is read out of freed memory whether or not it is
printed, and where that memory has been given out again the process dies
(SIGSEGV in av_log, from mediacodec_buffer_release; seen on Android when a
player opened its media again while the picture output still held pictures
of the decoder it closed).

With this those three lines name no context. Nothing else of the codec
context is read there: MediaCodec itself is the decoder context's, which
lasts as long as a buffer does.

--- a/libavcodec/mediacodec.c
+++ b/libavcodec/mediacodec.c
@@ -92,7 +92,10 @@
 
     if (!released && (ctx->delay_flush || buffer->serial == atomic_load(&ctx->serial))) {
         atomic_fetch_sub(&ctx->hw_buffer_count, 1);
-        av_log(ctx->avctx, AV_LOG_DEBUG,
+        /* Not ctx->avctx: a buffer can be let go of after its decoder
+         * was closed and its codec context freed, and av_log reads
+         * the context it is given. */
+        av_log(NULL, AV_LOG_DEBUG,
                "Releasing output buffer %zd (%p) ts=%"PRId64" with render=%d [%d pending]\n",
                buffer->index, buffer, buffer->pts, render, atomic_load(&ctx->hw_buffer_count));
         return ff_AMediaCodec_releaseOutputBuffer(ctx->codec, buffer->index, render);
@@ -108,7 +111,10 @@
 
     if (!released && (ctx->delay_flush || buffer->serial == atomic_load(&ctx->serial))) {
         atomic_fetch_sub(&ctx->hw_buffer_count, 1);
-        av_log(ctx->avctx, AV_LOG_DEBUG,
+        /* Not ctx->avctx: a buffer can be let go of after its decoder
+         * was closed and its codec context freed, and av_log reads
+         * the context it is given. */
+        av_log(NULL, AV_LOG_DEBUG,
                "Rendering output buffer %zd (%p) ts=%"PRId64" with time=%"PRId64" [%d pending]\n",
                buffer->index, buffer, buffer->pts, time, atomic_load(&ctx->hw_buffer_count));
         return ff_AMediaCodec_releaseOutputBufferAtTime(ctx->codec, buffer->index, time);
--- a/libavcodec/mediacodecdec_common.c
+++ b/libavcodec/mediacodecdec_common.c
@@ -284,7 +284,10 @@
 
     if (!released && (ctx->delay_flush || buffer->serial == atomic_load(&ctx->serial))) {
         atomic_fetch_sub(&ctx->hw_buffer_count, 1);
-        av_log(ctx->avctx, AV_LOG_DEBUG,
+        /* Not ctx->avctx: a buffer can be let go of after its decoder
+         * was closed and its codec context freed, and av_log reads
+         * the context it is given. */
+        av_log(NULL, AV_LOG_DEBUG,
                "Releasing output buffer %zd (%p) ts=%"PRId64" on free() [%d pending]\n",
                buffer->index, buffer, buffer->pts, atomic_load(&ctx->hw_buffer_count));
         ff_AMediaCodec_releaseOutputBuffer(ctx->codec, buffer->index, 0);
